### What is the Flip and Invert Image problem?

Given a binary matrix (containing only 0s and 1s), produce a matrix where each row is first reversed horizontally and then each bit is inverted (0 becomes 1, 1 becomes 0). The two operations can be fused into a single pass per row.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FlipAndInvert {
    public int[][] flipAndInvertImage(int[][] image) {
        for (int[] row : image) {
            int lo = 0, hi = row.length - 1;
            while (lo <= hi) {
                if (lo == hi) {
                    row[lo] ^= 1;
                } else {
                    int temp = row[lo] ^ 1;
                    row[lo] = row[hi] ^ 1;
                    row[hi] = temp;
                }
                lo++;
                hi--;
            }
        }
        return image;
    }
}
```

### Why does XOR with 1 invert a bit?

XOR with 1 flips a bit because of how XOR works: 0 XOR 1 = 1 (flips 0 to 1) and 1 XOR 1 = 0 (flips 1 to 0). This eliminates the need for a conditional check when inverting.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class XORInvertDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("0 ^ 1 = " + (0 ^ 1)); // 1
        System.out.println("1 ^ 1 = " + (1 ^ 1)); // 0

        // No conditional needed:
        int bit = 0;
        bit ^= 1; // now 1
        System.out.println("After invert: " + bit);
        bit ^= 1; // now 0
        System.out.println("After invert again: " + bit);
    }
}
```

```text
0 ^ 1 = 1
1 ^ 1 = 0
After invert: 1
After invert again: 0
```

### How do you fuse reverse and invert into one pass?

Instead of reversing the row first and then inverting in a second pass, we walk from both ends inward with two pointers. At each step, we place the inverted right value on the left and the inverted left value on the right. This does both operations simultaneously.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FusedPassDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] row = {1, 1, 0, 0};
        System.out.println("Before: " + java.util.Arrays.toString(row));

        int lo = 0, hi = row.length - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            if (lo == hi) {
                row[lo] ^= 1;
            } else {
                int temp = row[lo] ^ 1;
                row[lo] = row[hi] ^ 1;
                row[hi] = temp;
            }
            lo++; hi--;
        }
        System.out.println("After:  " + java.util.Arrays.toString(row));
        // Reverse of [1,1,0,0] is [0,0,1,1]
        // Invert: [1,1,0,0] -> that is the result
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [1, 1, 0, 0]
After:  [1, 1, 0, 0]
```

### How do you handle the middle element of an odd-length row?

When the row has an odd number of elements, the two pointers meet at the middle. This element does not need to be swapped with anything; it only needs to be inverted.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class OddLengthMiddle {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] row = {1, 0, 1}; // odd length
        int lo = 0, hi = 2;
        // Step 1: lo=0, hi=2 -> swap inverted
        int temp = row[0] ^ 1; // 0
        row[0] = row[2] ^ 1;   // 0
        row[2] = temp;          // 0
        // Step 2: lo=1, hi=1 -> middle, just invert
        row[1] ^= 1;           // 0 -> 1
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(row));
        // [0, 1, 0]
    }
}
```

```text
[0, 1, 0]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The fused approach visits each cell exactly once across all rows. No temporary matrix is needed because the operations are done in place.

- **Time complexity** – O(rows * cols), visiting each cell once.
- **Space complexity** – O(1) extra space beyond the input matrix.
- **Comparison** – a naive two-pass approach (reverse then invert) visits each cell twice and may allocate a temporary row.

```java
// Fused: one pass per row, O(1) extra space
for (int[] row : image) {
    int lo = 0, hi = row.length - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) { /* fused reverse + invert */ }
}

// Naive: two passes per row
for (int[] row : image) {
    Collections.reverse(row);  // pass 1
    for (int j = 0; j < row.length; j++) row[j] ^= 1; // pass 2
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is not handling the odd-length middle element, causing it to be either double-inverted (returning to original) or not inverted at all. Another mistake is creating a temporary matrix when one is not needed.

```java
// WRONG: middle element double-processed
while (lo < hi) {
    // this skips the middle element entirely!
    int temp = row[lo] ^ 1;
    row[lo] = row[hi] ^ 1;
    row[hi] = temp;
    lo++; hi--;
}
// Middle element at lo==hi is never inverted!

// CORRECT: include lo == hi case
while (lo <= hi) {
    if (lo == hi) {
        row[lo] ^= 1; // only invert, no swap
    } else {
        int temp = row[lo] ^ 1;
        row[lo] = row[hi] ^ 1;
        row[hi] = temp;
    }
    lo++; hi--;
}
```

### How is this different from a simple matrix transpose?

Flip and Invert operates within each row independently (horizontal flip + invert). A matrix transpose swaps rows and columns across the entire matrix. They are fundamentally different operations.

| Aspect | Flip and Invert | Matrix Transpose |
|---|---|---|
| Scope | Per-row operation | Whole-matrix operation |
| Reverse direction | Horizontal (left-right) | Rows become columns |
| Inversion | Yes (XOR with 1) | No |
| Matrix shape | Rows unchanged | Rows and cols swap |

```java
// Flip and Invert: per row
for (int[] row : matrix) { /* reverse and invert row */ }

// Transpose: swap matrix[i][j] and matrix[j][i]
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
        int temp = matrix[i][j];
        matrix[i][j] = matrix[j][i];
        matrix[j][i] = temp;
    }
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a cheque-scanning module stores each scanned line as a binary row. A legacy reader expects every line mirrored and inverted. The fused reverse-and-invert pass processes large scan batches without allocating a second matrix.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ChequeScanProcessor {
    public int[][] processForLegacyReader(int[][] scanLines) {
        for (int[] line : scanLines) {
            int lo = 0, hi = line.length - 1;
            while (lo <= hi) {
                if (lo == hi) {
                    line[lo] ^= 1;
                } else {
                    int temp = line[lo] ^ 1;
                    line[lo] = line[hi] ^ 1;
                    line[hi] = temp;
                }
                lo++; hi--;
            }
        }
        return scanLines;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognise that reverse and invert are commutative cell-wise: reversing then inverting gives the same result as inverting then reversing. This commutativity is what allows fusing them into one pass. They should also note that when `lo != hi` and `row[lo] == row[hi]`, the swap-then-invert has no net effect (both values stay the same), which could be used as a minor optimisation.

```java
// Optimisation: if row[lo] == row[hi], the fused op is a no-op
if (lo != hi && row[lo] == row[hi]) {
    // reverse swaps them, then invert flips both -> net effect: no change
    lo++; hi--;
    continue;
}
// Otherwise, do the full fused swap-and-invert
```
